{#ch11-0001}
# The Sayings of Alvíss

{#ch11-0002 ref="alv.1"}
::: stanza
‘Now shall a bride cover[^2] the benches with me,[^3]
come home in my company;
it’ll seem to everyone a hurried engagement,[^4]
she shan’t take her leisure at home!’[^5]
:::

{#ch11-0003 ref="alv.2"}
::: stanza
‘What kind of living thing is that? Why are you so livid around the nose?
Were you with a corpse last night?[^6]
There seems to me to be the likeness of giants in you,[^7]
you weren’t born for a bride!’
:::

{#ch11-0004 ref="alv.3"}
::: stanza
‘I am named Alvíss,[^8] I live beneath the earth,
I have a homestead under stone;[^9]
I’ve come to see the man of carts(?),[^10]
let no one break people’s firm pledge!’
:::

{#ch11-0005 ref="alv.4"}
::: stanza
‘I shall break it, because I have most say
over the bride, like her father;[^11]
I wasn’t at home when she was betrothed to you,
\[I being\] the only one who is her giver(?) among the gods.’[^12]
:::

{#ch11-0006 ref="alv.5"}
::: stanza
‘What kind of man are you who claims authority
over the fair-glowing woman?
Few will recognize you, vagrant[^13] —
who has born you to arm-rings?’[^14]
:::

{#ch11-0007 ref="alv.6"}
::: stanza
‘I am named Vingþórr [^15] — I have wandered widely[^16] —
I am Síðgrani’s[^17] son;
not with my consent shall you have the young maiden
and get that marriage.’[^18]
:::

{#ch11-0008 ref="alv.7"}
::: stanza
‘Your consent is what I soon want to have,
and to make this marriage;
I would rather have, than be without,
that snow-white[^19] maiden.’
:::

{#ch11-0009 ref="alv.8"}
::: stanza
‘The maiden’s love won’t be withheld
from you, wise guest,[^20]
if you can tell me all that I want to know
from every world.[^21]
:::

{#ch11-0010 ref="alv.9"}
::: stanza
‘Tell me this, Alvíss — I expect, dwarf, that you know
all the history of living beings:
what the earth,[^22] which lies before the sons of men,
is named in each world.’
:::

{#ch11-0011 ref="alv.10"}
::: stanza
‘It’s named {_jǫrð_} [^23] among men, but {_fold_} [^24] among Æsir,
Vanir call it {_vegar_},[^25]
giants {_ígrœn_},[^26] elves {_gróandi_},[^27]
the powers above[^28] call it {_aurr_}.’[^29]
:::

{#ch11-0012 ref="alv.11"}
::: stanza
‘Tell me this, Alvíss — I expect, dwarf, that you know
all the history of living beings:
what the sky, the one known to Hymir(?),[^30]
is named in each world.’
:::

{#ch11-0013 ref="alv.12"}
::: stanza
‘It’s named {_himinn_} [^31] among men, but {_hlýrnir_} [^32] among gods,
Vanir call it {_vindofnir_},[^33]
giants {_uppheimr_},[^34] elves {_fagrarræfr_},[^35]
dwarves {_drjúpr salr_}.’[^36]
:::

{#ch11-0014 ref="alv.13"}
::: stanza
‘Tell me this, Alvíss — I expect, dwarf, that you know
all the history of living beings:
how the moon, which men see,
is named in each world.’
:::

{#ch11-0015 ref="alv.14"}
::: stanza
‘It’s named {_máni_} [^37] among men, but {_mýlinn_} [^38] among gods,
in Hel they call it {_hverfanda hvél_},[^39]
giants {_skyndir_},[^40] and dwarves {_skin_},[^41]
elves call it {_ártali_}.’[^42]
:::

{#ch11-0016 ref="alv.15"}
::: stanza
‘Tell me this, Alvíss — I expect, dwarf, that you know
all the history of living beings:
what the sun, which the sons of men see,
is named in each world.’
:::

{#ch11-0017 ref="alv.16"}
::: stanza
‘It’s named {_sól_} among men, but {_sunna_} [^43] among gods,
dwarves call it {_Dvalin_} {_s leiki_},[^44]
giants {_eygló_},[^45] elves {_fagrahvél_},[^46]
sons of the Æsir[^47] {_alskír_}.’[^48]
:::

{#ch11-0018 ref="alv.17"}
::: stanza
‘Tell me this, Alvíss — I expect, dwarf, that you know
all the history of living beings:
what the clouds, which mix with showers,
are named in each world.’
:::

{#ch11-0019 ref="alv.18"}
::: stanza
‘They’re named {_ský_} [^49] among men, but {_skúrván_} [^50] among gods,
Vanir call them {_vindflot_};[^51]
giants {_úrván_},[^52] elves {_veðrmegin_},[^53]
in Hel they call them {_hjálmr huliðs_}.’[^54]
:::

{#ch11-0020 ref="alv.19"}
::: stanza
‘Tell me this, Alvíss — I expect, dwarf, that you know
all the history of living beings:
what the wind, which travels most widely,
is named in each world.’
:::

{#ch11-0021 ref="alv.20"}
::: stanza
‘It’s named {_vindr_} [^55] among men, but {_váfuðr_} [^56] among gods,
mighty powers[^57] call it {_gneggjuðr_},[^58]
giants {_œpir_},[^59] elves {_dynfari_},[^60]
in Hel they call it {_hviðuðr_}.’[^61]
:::

{#ch11-0022 ref="alv.21"}
::: stanza
‘Tell me this, Alvíss — I expect, dwarf, that you know
all the history of living beings:
what the calm, which shall lie,[^62]
is named in each world.’
:::

{#ch11-0023 ref="alv.22"}
::: stanza
‘It’s named {_logn_} [^63] among men, but {_lœgi_} [^64] among gods,
Vanir call it {_vindslot_},[^65]
giants {_ofhlý_},[^66] elves {_dagsefi_},[^67]
dwarves call it {_dags vera_}.’[^68]
:::

{#ch11-0024 ref="alv.23"}
::: stanza
‘Tell me this, Alvíss — I expect, dwarf, that you know
all the history of living beings:
what the sea, which men row on,
is named in each world.’
:::

{#ch11-0025 ref="alv.24"}
::: stanza
‘It’s named {_sær_} [^69] among men, but {_sílægja_} [^70] among gods,
Vanir call it {_vágr_},[^71]
giants {_álheimr_},[^72] elves {_lagastafr_},[^73]
dwarves call it {_djúpr marr_}.’[^74]
:::

{#ch11-0026 ref="alv.25"}
::: stanza
‘Tell me this, Alvíss — I expect, dwarf, that you know
all the history of living beings:
what the fire, which burns before the sons of men,
is named in each world.’
:::

{#ch11-0027 ref="alv.26"}
::: stanza
‘It’s named {_eldr_} [^75] among men, but {_funi_} [^76] among Æsir,
Vanir call it {_vágr_},[^77]
giants {_freki_},[^78] and dwarves {_forbrennir_},[^79]
in Hel they call it {_hrǫðuðr_}.’[^80]
:::

{#ch11-0028 ref="alv.27"}
::: stanza
‘Tell me this, Alvíss — I expect, dwarf, that you know
all the history of living beings:
what the wood, which grows before the sons of men,
is named in each world.’
:::

{#ch11-0029 ref="alv.28"}
::: stanza
‘It’s named {_viðr_} [^81] among men, but {_vallar fax_} [^82] by gods,
heroes[^83] call it {_hlíðþang_},[^84]
giants {_eldi_},[^85] elves {_fagrlimi_},[^86]
Vanir call it {_vǫndr_}.’[^87]
:::

{#ch11-0030 ref="alv.29"}
::: stanza
‘Tell me this, Alvíss — I expect, dwarf, that you know
all the history of living beings:
what the night, the one known to Nǫrr,[^88]
is named in each world.’
:::

{#ch11-0031 ref="alv.30"}
::: stanza
‘It’s named {_nótt_} [^89] among men, but {_njól_} [^90] among gods,
mighty powers[^91] call it {_gríma_},[^92]
giants {_óljós_},[^93] elves {_svefngaman_},[^94]
dwarves call it {_draumnjǫrun_}.’[^95]
:::

{#ch11-0032 ref="alv.31"}
::: stanza
‘Tell me this, Alvíss — I expect, dwarf, that you know
all the history of living beings:
what the seed, which the sons of men sow,
is named in each world.’
:::

{#ch11-0033 ref="alv.32"}
::: stanza
‘It’s named {_bygg_} [^96] among men, but {_barr_} [^97] among gods,
Vanir call it {_vaxt_},[^98]
giants {_æti_},[^99] elves {_lagastafr_},[^100]
in Hel they call it {_hnipinn_}.’[^101]
:::

{#ch11-0034 ref="alv.33"}
::: stanza
‘Tell me this, Alvíss — I expect, dwarf, that you know
all the history of living beings:
what the ale,[^102] which the sons of men drink,
is named in each world.’
:::

{#ch11-0035 ref="alv.34"}
::: stanza
‘It’s named {_ǫl_} [^103] among men, but {_bjórr_} [^104] by Æsir,
Vanir call it {_veig_},[^105]
giants {_hreinalǫgr_},[^106] and in Hel {_mjǫðr_},[^107]
Suttungr’s sons[^108] call it {_sumbl_}.’[^109]
:::

{#ch11-0036 ref="alv.35"}
::: stanza
‘In a single breast I’ve never seen
more ancient staves\![^110]
By great tricks I declare you’ve been duped:
you’re up, dwarf, when it’s dawned,[^111]
the sun now shines in the halls!’[^112]
:::

[^2]: Or perhaps ‘prepare’.

[^3]: Hosts covered their benches with cushions or straw in preparation for guests; cf. {_Þrk._} 22.

[^4]: Literally, ‘relationship by marriage’.

[^5]: This line is ambiguous. It could mean that Alvíss will not let his hastily acquired bride live quietly at {_her_} home among the gods, but will rather put her to work at {_his_} home. Or it could be a sexual boast that ‘at my home she shan’t get any rest!’ Another possibility is ‘at my home no one shall deprive us of rest’.

[^6]: Dwarves live below ground, where the dead also reside.

[^7]: Norse giants and dwarves have affinities: both live in rocks; {_Vsp._} 9 says dwarves came from the blood of the giant Brimir and the limbs of the giant Bláinn; {_SnEGylf_} (14, p. 15) says dwarves originated as maggots in the flesh of the giant Ymir; {_Þórsdrápa_}, in {_SnESkáld_} (I, 18, p. 29), calls the giant Geirrøðr {_áttruðr Suðri_} ‘relative of Suðri \[a dwarf\]’; wisdom is a characteristic of some giants; they share such names as Fjalarr and Fjǫlsviðr. Reginn, foster-father of Sigurðr, is described as {_dvergr of vǫxt_} ‘a dwarf in stature’ in the prose prologue to {_Rm._}, but as a {_jǫtunn_} ‘giant’ in {_Fm._} 38.

[^8]: ‘All Wise’.

[^9]: The usual dwelling place of dwarves; cf. e.g., {_Vsp._} 50.

[^10]: Or ‘I’ve come to see the sea of carts \[i.e., the surface of the earth\]’, given that dwarves live below ground. Although Þórr drives a chariot (cf. {_Þrk._} 21), if ‘man of carts’ is the (or an) intended sense here, it may instead describe Óðinn, who is called {_vagna rúni_} ‘friend of wagons’ in st. 22 of Egill Skalla-Grímsson’s skaldic poem {_Sonatorrek_} ‘The Wreck of Sons’. It may be that Óðinn promised a woman—possibly Þórr’s foster-daughter—to Alvíss during one of Þórr’s frequent absences.

[^11]: The girl might have been entrusted to Þórr’s care. It is doubtful whether {_sem faðir_} might alternatively mean ‘as (i.e., in my capacity as) her father’, in which case the bride would be Þórr’s biological daughter and at least partly divine.

[^12]: I.e., Þórr is the only god who has the right to give her away. But the last line of this stanza is problematic and its precise meaning unclear.

[^13]: Cf\. Þórr’s beggarly appearance as a traveller in {_Hrbl._} 6.

[^14]: A problematic line, but probably a riposte to Þórr’s opinion that Alvíss’s birth disqualifies him from marriage. Alvíss assumes that the ‘vagrant’ is not of noble birth, and that he therefore cannot have the authority he claims. This line could also be interpreted as {_hverr hefir þik baug um borit?_} ‘who has brought you an arm-ring?’—an accusation of penury that might imply that Þórr would have to rely on charity for his daughter’s dowry.

[^15]: Probably ‘Swinging-Þórr’.

[^16]: Þórr’s journeys invariably involve slaying giants.

[^17]: ‘Broad Moustache’, an alias of Óðinn.

[^18]: Literally, ‘gift-word’, i.e., verbal permission for the ‘gift’ of a female in marriage.

[^19]: Or ‘flour-white’.

[^20]: A designation presumably deduced from the meaning of the dwarf’s name: ‘All Wise’. Cf. {_Vm._} 19.

[^21]: ‘World’ (literally ‘home’) as in ‘region inhabited by a specific type of being’. In many paper manuscripts this stanza is followed by another in which Alvíss invites Þórr to test his knowledge and claims to have travelled through all nine worlds.

[^22]: Þórr, perhaps in keeping with his role as Miðgarðr’s protector and god of farmers, generally uses the language of humans (‘men’) in {_Alv_}.

[^23]: ‘Earth’.

[^24]: ‘Field’.

[^25]: ‘Ways’.

[^26]: ‘Greenish’ or perhaps ‘inherently/very green’.

[^27]: ‘Growing’.

[^28]: The identity of the {_uppregin,_} literally ‘up-powers’, is unclear; cf. {_vís regin_} in {_Vm._} 39 and {_ginnregin_} in {_Alv._} 20.

[^29]: ‘Clay’ or ‘mud’, ‘loam’.

[^30]: The unemended text ({_erakendi_}) is corrupt here. The likely parallel of {_Alv._} 29 {_in Nǫrvi kenda_} suggests that a proper noun alliterating on {_h-_} has been omitted. Hymir was a giant who, according to {_Hym._} 5, lived {_at himins enda_} ‘at the sky’s end’; his presence here, being dependent on an emendation, is conjectural.

[^31]: ‘Sky/heaven’.

[^32]: Literally, ‘(one with) faced heavenly bodies (i.e., sun and moon)’.

[^33]: ‘Wind-weaver’.

[^34]: ‘Up-world’.

[^35]: ‘Fair roof’.

[^36]: ‘Dripping hall’. Cf. the list of names for the sky in {_SnESkáld_} (I, 56, p. 85).

[^37]: ‘Moon’.

[^38]: Possibly a term for the waning (crescent) moon.

[^39]: ‘Whirling wheel’.

[^40]: ‘Hurrier’. Cf. {_Grm._} 39 and {_SnEGylf_} (12, p. 14 and p. 60 n.), according to which the moon is pursued by the wolf Hati Hróðvitnisson.

[^41]: ‘Shine’.

[^42]: ‘Year-counter’. Cf. {_Vsp._} 6 and {_Vm._} 25.

[^43]: ‘Sun’.

[^44]: ‘Dvalinn’s toy/plaything’ or ‘Dvalinn’s playmate’. Dwarves were probably believed to turn to stone, or at least to be destroyed in some way, if they were above ground during the day. It is likely that Dvalinn, a dwarf whose name is interpretable as ‘Delayed (One)’, played with at least a piece of stolen sun too long and paid the price at dawn.

[^45]: ‘Ever-glow’.

[^46]: ‘Fair wheel’.

[^47]: The ‘sons of the Æsir’ are perhaps just Æsir.

[^48]: ‘All-pure/bright’. Cf. the lists of names for the sun in {_SnESkáld_} (I, 56, p. 85; 75, p. 133).

[^49]: ‘Clouds’.

[^50]: ‘Prospect of shower’.

[^51]: ‘Wind-floating’.

[^52]: ‘Prospect of drizzle’.

[^53]: ‘Weather/wind/storm power’.

[^54]: ‘Helmet of hiding’.

[^55]: ‘Wind’.

[^56]: ‘Waverer’ or perhaps ‘roamer’. Elsewhere this term is an alias of Óðinn.

[^57]: It is unclear who the ‘mighty powers’ are.

[^58]: ‘Neigher’.

[^59]: ‘Howler’.

[^60]: ‘Din-farer’.

[^61]: ‘Squaller’. {_SnESkáld_} (I, 59, p. 90) quotes a variant version of this stanza.

[^62]: This line is metrically suspect; possibly {_fyr alda sonum_} ‘before the sons of men’ has dropped out.

[^63]: ‘Calm’.

[^64]: ‘Laying(?)’.

[^65]: ‘Wind-end’.

[^66]: ‘Sultry’.

[^67]: ‘Day-soother’.

[^68]: ‘Day’s existence/shelter’.

[^69]: ‘Sea’.

[^70]: ‘Ever-calm’ or perhaps ‘ever-lying’.

[^71]: ‘Surge’. Cf. {_Alv._} 26.

[^72]: ‘Eel-home’ or perhaps ‘home of the ocean-current’.

[^73]: ‘Waters’ stave/(source?)’, a puzzling term.

[^74]: ‘Deep sea’.

[^75]: ‘Fire’.

[^76]: ‘Flame’.

[^77]: ‘Surge’. Cf. {_Alv._} 24.

[^78]: ‘Greedy’.

[^79]: ‘Burner up’.

[^80]: ‘Hurrier’.

[^81]: ‘Wood’.

[^82]: ‘Field’s mane’. Cf. {_Grm._} 40.

[^83]: The distinction from ‘men’ is unclear.

[^84]: ‘Slope-seaweed’.

[^85]: ‘Firewood’.

[^86]: ‘Fair-limb’.

[^87]: ‘Wand’. Or, given the Vanir’s association with the sea, ‘mast’, a frequent meaning of {_vǫndr_} in skaldic poetry.

[^88]: Nǫrr is the father of night; cf. {_Vm._} 25.

[^89]: ‘Night’.

[^90]: ‘Darkness’.

[^91]: Cf\. {_Alv._} 20.

[^92]: ‘Mask’.

[^93]: ‘Unlight’.

[^94]: ‘Sleep-joy’.

[^95]: ‘Dream-Njǫrun’. Njǫrun is a goddess whose name appears in kennings for ‘woman’. {_SnESkáld_} (I, 63, p. 99) quotes a variant version of this stanza.

[^96]: ‘Barley’.

[^97]: ‘Barley’.

[^98]: ‘Growth’. Or {_vaxtr_}.

[^99]: ‘Food’.

[^100]: ‘Waters’ stave/(source?)’. {_Lagastafr_} makes no sense here; it is presumably a mistaken scribal repetition from {_Alv._} 24, where it is the elves’ word for ‘sea’. The elves’ word for ‘seed’ is apparently lost.

[^101]: ‘Hanging down’.

[^102]: Or ‘beer’. Þórr follows a question about ‘seed’/‘grain’ with one about ale presumably because malt was used in the brewing process.

[^103]: ‘Ale’.

[^104]: ‘Beer’.

[^105]: ‘Liquor’.

[^106]: ‘Bright liquid’.

[^107]: ‘Mead’.

[^108]: Giants again. Suttungr was a giant who owned the mead of poetry; see {_Háv._} 104, 109 and {_FSk._} 34 (Suttungi).

[^109]: ‘Drink’.

[^110]: I.e., (rune-)staves, which represent wisdom (cf. {_Vm._} 55). Here the term probably also alludes to the sun’s rays, imagined as piercing staff-like weapons.

[^111]: Literally, ‘you’re dayed up, dwarf’. Alvíss is above ground at daybreak.

[^112]: Alvíss is presumably destroyed, probably by petrification, having been tricked by Þórr into being above ground at dawn. Cf. {_HHv._} 29–30, {_Hdl._} 49(?).

